##
    Functions left open, and the promises that go with them.

    This program does not compile. An 'abstract' function is a name and a signature with
    nothing behind them, so it only means something if nobody can construct the model carrying
    it, and if the obligation to write it eventually reaches a model somebody can construct.

    Every mistake here is one of those two rules breaking, plus the two that keep a body and
    the word 'abstract' agreeing with each other.
##

##
    A model anyone can construct, holding a function nobody wrote. Constructing it would reach
    Area and find nothing there.
##
model Loose
    public abstract real function Area();
end model

##
    The two directions of the same rule. A function without 'abstract' has to say what it does,
    because nothing obliges anyone else to; a function with it must not, because every model
    below writes its own and this one would never run.
##
abstract model Muddled
    public real function Perimeter();

    public abstract real function Area()
        yield 0.0;
    end function
end model

##
    The obligation arriving somewhere it is not met. Solid can be constructed, so it has to
    write both of the functions Shape left open, and it writes neither.
##
abstract model Shape
    public abstract real function Area();
    public abstract string function Name();
end model

model Solid extends Shape
end model

shared model Program
    function Main()
        Console.WriteLine(new Loose().Area());
    end function
end model
